# The ligature tree PAX carries as /usr/bin/ligature in the dev image
# (px128; tools/mkligature builds it).
#
# ligature is the linker in wolf (wolffe-lang/ligature, the linker track):
# static x86-64 ELF for Linux and PAX, refusal-first, a byte-and-patch
# provenance manifest in every output. Its cc front end takes the link
# line `wolf build` gives a selected linker (`WOLF_LD`, `--linker`) and
# reads musl's start files and libc from /usr/lib/wolf-musl, where the dev
# image keeps them (user/wolf-link.sums). Its clean room is the ELF gABI,
# the x86-64 psABI and the linkers' manuals; PAX reads no more of its tree
# than building it needs.
#
# On the dev image ligature is an opt-in beside lld, never the default:
# `export WOLF_LD=/usr/bin/ligature` selects it (and silences wolf's note
# naming lld). A pelt-sized link needs about 750 MiB resident today, more
# than the 2 GiB guest the dev image is tested with leaves (ligature#35);
# it links at 2560 MiB.
#
# ligature trunk 32ec7f4 (2026-11-10): the wolf 0.2.38 pin and its link
# bundle (ligature#34), the link's memory bounded (ligature#33). Pinned in
# its own wolf-toolchain.toml at wolf 0.2.38 / lupin 0.1.60 / wolf-std
# d4a4d4ca (the std the wolf archive bundles). tools/mkligature builds it
# with THAT toolchain, as ligature's tools/build does, through a cc that
# adds -static and no build-id and links with GNU ld, in two checkouts,
# refusing two digests, and ships the stripped copy. The digest below is
# the one mkligature prints on an Ubuntu 24.04 container (glibc 2.39), and
# tests/distribution holds the image to it (recorded below once built).
LIGATURE_REPO=https://github.com/wolffe-lang/ligature
LIGATURE_REV=32ec7f435fe45e6e7f7fae921b1bbd180762f6d3
